#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135048 :tutorial/content "If $\\big\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 , \\ldots, \\mathbf{a}_n \\big\\}$ is a set of orthogonal vectors, then the **orthogonal projection of a vector $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the subspace $S = \\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 , \\ldots, \\mathbf{a}_n \\}$** is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x+⋯+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{n}}x \\\\  & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}+\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2}+⋯+\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{n}}{{a}_{n}\\cdot {a}_{n}}{a}_{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, the orthogonal projection of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto $S = \\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 , \\ldots, \\mathbf{a}_n \\}$ is the sum of the orthogonal projections of $\\mathbf{x}$ onto each one-dimensional subspace of $\\textrm{Span}\\big\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 , \\ldots, \\mathbf{a}_n \\big\\}.$\n\n**Watch out!** The formula works *only* when the set of vectors $\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 , \\ldots, \\mathbf{a}_n \\}$ is orthogonal!\n\nIn case of a $2$-dimensional subspace $S=\\textrm{Span} \\big\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2 \\big\\},$ we can visualize the orthogonal projection geometrically, as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}\\textrm{proj}_{S} \\: \\mathbf{x}} \n\t= \n\t{\\color{red}\\textrm{proj}_{\\mathbf{a}_1}\\mathbf{x}} + {\\color{red}\\textrm{proj}_{\\mathbf{a}_2}\\mathbf{x}}\n$$\n\n![](images/7d/7d27747451504c7244ddac4609c9c205c60cd525de2cfd364c20e90c1655ef4e.png)\n\n**Note:** A proof of the formula will be provided at the end of the lesson. But first, let's get some practice using the formula." :tutorial/id #uuid "52ad8222-2949-5d1f-b34c-ea06e2a98ddc" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3625 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135087 :tutorial/content "Let's now prove the formula for the orthogonal projection of a vector $\\mathbf{x}$ onto the subspaces $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1 \\}$ and $\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_2 \\}$ given that $\\mathbf{a}_1\\perp \\mathbf{a}_2.$ \n\nWe start by writing $\\mathbf{x}$ as a sum of two vectors\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{x}_S + \\mathbf{x}_{S^\\perp},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{x}_S=\\text{proj}_S\\,\\mathbf{x}$ is in $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2\\},$ and $\\mathbf{x}_{S^\\perp}$ is in the orthogonal complement of $\\textrm{Span}\\{\\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2\\}$. That is, $\\mathbf{x}_S=k_1\\mathbf{a}_1+k_2\\mathbf{a}_2\\in S\\,$ and $\\mathbf{x}_{S^\\perp} \\in S^\\perp.$\n\nFrom the equation above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{{S}^{⊥}} & =x-{x}_{S} \\\\  & =x-({k}_{1}{a}_{1}+{k}_{2}{a}_{2}) \\\\  & =x-{k}_{1}{a}_{1}-{k}_{2}{a}_{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that $\\mathbf{x}_{S^\\perp}$ is orthogonal to $S,$ and the fact that $\\mathbf{a}_1\\cdot\\mathbf{a}_2=0,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{{S}^{⊥}}\\cdot {a}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{{S}^{⊥}}\\cdot {a}_{2}=0\\end{aligned} & \\,\\,\\,⟹\\,\\,\\,{\\begin{aligned}(x-{k}_{1}{a}_{1}-{k}_{2}{a}_{2})\\cdot {a}_{1}=0 \\\\ (x-{k}_{1}{a}_{1}-{k}_{2}{a}_{2})\\cdot {a}_{2}=0\\end{aligned} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,⟹\\,\\,\\,{\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot {a}_{1}-{k}_{1}({a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1})-{k}_{2}({a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{1})=0 \\\\ x\\cdot {a}_{2}-{k}_{1}({a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{2})-{k}_{2}({a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2})=0\\end{aligned} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,⟹\\,\\,\\,{\\begin{aligned}{k}_{1}({a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1})=x\\cdot {a}_{1} \\\\ {k}_{2}({a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2})=x\\cdot {a}_{2}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,⟹\\,\\,\\,{\\begin{aligned}{k}_{1}=\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}} \\\\ {k}_{2}=\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain our formula \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & ={k}_{1}{a}_{1}+{k}_{2}{a}_{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{1}}{{a}_{1}\\cdot {a}_{1}}{a}_{1}+\\frac{x\\cdot {a}_{2}}{{a}_{2}\\cdot {a}_{2}}{a}_{2} \\\\  & ={\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{1}}x+{\\text{proj}}_{{a}_{2}}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "adabdbc0-f726-5b67-81e5-73ef172ea7cf" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4345 :tutorial/title "Justification of the Formula"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186135088 :tutorial/content "Let $n,m$ be positive integers such that $n\\leq m.$ Given an orthogonal set $\\big\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1, \\mathbf{a}_2 , \\ldots, \\mathbf{a}_n \\big\\} \\subset  \\Bbb R^m$ and a vector $\\mathbf x \\in \\Bbb R^m,$ we define the **distance between the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and the vector space $S=\\textrm{Span}\\{ \\mathbf{a}_1,\\mathbf{a}_2,  \\ldots, \\mathbf{a}_n \\}$** as\n\n$$\n\\Vert{ \\color{purple} \\mathbf{x} - \\text{proj}_{S}\\,\\mathbf{x} }\\Vert.\n$$\n\nWe can also define the **angle between the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and the subspace $S$** to be the acute angle $\\theta$ between the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ and its orthogonal projection $\\text{proj}_{S}\\,\\mathbf{x},$ as shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/07/07fa0d011bbfd49f89ddc5035a27839640a0c2624046d39fbfe8f57c476b5b25.png)\n\nFinally, notice that we can represent the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ as a sum of two orthogonal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} = \\underbrace{\\textrm{proj}_{S}\\mathbf{x}}_{\\large \\in S} + \\underbrace{(\\mathbf{x}-\\textrm{proj}_{S}\\mathbf{x})}_{\\large \\in S^\\perp}\n$$\n\n \n\n**Note:** The vector $\\textbf{x}-\\textrm{proj}_{S}\\,\\mathbf{x},$ whose norm $\\| \\textbf{x}-\\textrm{proj}_{S}\\,\\mathbf{x} \\|$ represents the distance from $\\mathbf{x}$ to $S,$ is sometimes called the **vector rejection of $\\mathbf{x}$ from $S.$**" :tutorial/id #uuid "0c1861f6-1be0-5e7e-88be-6186634715ef" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4346 :tutorial/title "The Distance Between a Vector and a Subspace"}]}
